{"kind":"artifact","text":"The adapter judges the resident record on every durable act, not once per session. Exact clean pushed head 128132f23c3ea13e7b0ed93e33735f6181531c92 on request/mcp-advert-durable-refusal; tree unchanged from 9d3e6483, checkout clean, origin matching. Under planner's child 467ad483 and my promise 5ee01d7b.\n\nWHAT THE DEFECT WAS. The refusal only ran while the room had no address. A room that had already found a resident kept the address it remembered and never read the record again, so a record rewritten an hour into a session was ignored; and when the service it named then stopped, the transport error dropped the act into the local fold with the tampering unmentioned. The guard protected a fresh room and let a working one straight through — which is the case a tamper is most likely to find.\n\nWHERE IT IS FIXED, re-derived at this head rather than carried from the earlier review. resolveEndpoint at line 176 takes a classify argument, and that argument is the whole difference between the two callers: false asks what address this room is using, answered from the cache without touching disk, which keeps the read path as cheap as it was; true asks what the record says now, which is the only question a durable act may act on. durableEndpoint at 216 asks the second. In submit, line 1163 calls it and line 1167 reads the signing key — that order is the security property, not an accident of layout. Line 1195 reads the record once more after transport loss, because a rewrite can land while a call is in flight and a stopped service is what that looks like from here.\n\nAbsence stays absence. A repository advertising nothing acts locally as it always did, and a room already holding an address keeps it, since a service that has gone will say so by not answering.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and watched rather than asserted. Three mutations, three kills, worktree restored clean afterwards. Moving the durableEndpoint call below the key read fails TestUntrustedAdvertisementRefusesBeforeTheSigningKeyIsRead with \"the signing key was read before the advertisement was judged; the refusal came from the key, not the record\". Disabling the post-transport-loss re-check fails TestAdvertisementRewrittenDuringACallRefusesTheLocalFallback with \"the act was folded locally after the record was rewritten while the call was in flight\". Dropping classify so the cache short-circuits the re-read fails TestUntrustedAdvertisementRefusesADurableActAfterAGoodOneWasCached across four of its six subtests. Seven tests were added in untrusted_advertisement_test.go; these three pin the properties that are easiest to lose silently.\n\nGATES, from runs I watched exit at this exact head. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 35 packages, 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 318.689s, internal/kernel 245.744s, internal/app 243.478s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 206.823s, internal/docset 172.454s. npm test 87 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty, so the embed is byte-reproduced; make ui-check exit 0. The first UI run in this worktree failed five files with \"Cannot find module react\" — that was a missing node_modules, not this change; npm ci then 87/87. I am naming that rather than quietly reporting only the second run.","body":{"commit":"128132f23c3ea13e7b0ed93e33735f6181531c92","path":"cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}